Then reacts from a PDArray
Then confirms with SSMT
Then expands during the expected distribution phase
PDArray preference
Prefer BPRs over FVGs
NWOG aligned with FVG after SSMT strengthens the reaction case
7) Confirmation Rules
SMT / SSMT
SMT kicks off the move
Sequential SMT confirms manipulation
SSMT is especially important across quarter transitions, especially Q3–Q4
Close-based confirmation
Closes matter more than wicks
Wicks are treated more like gaps or probing behavior
Stronger confirmation comes from one asset closing through a key level while the correlated asset does not
PSP logic
A PSP does not need to be a swing point on all assets
If it is a swing point on at least one asset, it can still qualify
A non-hidden SSMT that only wicks past prior-quarter levels matters when that candle closes as a PSP
8) Primary Trade Models
A) True Open manipulation model
Price trades below/above the relevant True Open
It fails to continue cleanly
It reclaims or rejects around the True Open
SSMT confirms relative weakness/strength
Entry is taken toward the expected distribution leg
B) Q4 reversal model
Requirements:
Swing point forms in the second half of Q3
SSMT appears in the first half of Q4
Price is above the Revolving True Open for bearish reversal logic, or inverse for bullish logic
Favor the asset that failed to make the confirming high/low
C) News-driven manipulation/distribution model
Manipulation often occurs before the major news release
If manipulation already happened, the news event often becomes distribution
LTF SSMT should appear in the quarter where the news occurs
If price runs without the LTF SSMT, probability drops and the trade is often skipped
9) News Timing Rules
9:30 rule
If manipulation occurs before 9:30, expect 9:30 distribution
If no manipulation occurs before 9:30, expect 9:30 manipulation
If 9:30 manipulates, expect roughly 10:00 expansion
If 9:30 distributes, expect 10:00 retracement or reversal
