Thought for 14s
Yes, but “different market conditions” is only half the problem.
The real issue
A strategy can only trade rules that are explicit, binary, and testable.
Your model still contains a lot of discretionary language, which humans handle well and code handles badly.

Examples:

“key PDArray”
“proper quarter timing”
“manipulation”
“good SSMT”
“crack in correlation”
“best asset to trade”
“news likely becomes distribution”
That sounds clean in your head, but to a strategy it means: define the exact formula or I do nothing.
What code needs that your model does not yet fully have
1. Precise definitions
You need exact answers to questions like:
What is a swing high/low?
What qualifies as PSP?
What exact candle behavior counts as SSMT?
How many bars apart can the two assets be?
Does wick count, close count, or both?
Which PDArray has priority if two are present?
If those are not mathematically fixed, the backtest is garbage.
2. Regime handling
Yes, market conditions matter.
But the real coding problem is not “markets change.”

It is that your setup likely behaves differently in different regimes, and the code does not yet know when to switch gears.

Examples:

trend day vs range day
news day vs non-news day
expansion already done vs expansion pending
correlated assets in sync vs cracked correlation
clean delivery vs sloppy chop
A human can say, “this is not the right environment.”
A dumb strategy says, “signal fired, boss.”

3. Multi-layer context
Your model is not a single trigger.
It is a stacked narrative model:

quarter of cycle
relative location to True Open
PDArray interaction
SMT/SSMT
intermarket confirmation
news timing
expected distribution window
That is codeable, but it becomes a state machine, not a simple entry condition.
4. Ambiguous sequencing
A lot of your edge is about order of events, not just presence of events.
Example:

manipulate first
then SSMT
then reclaim/reject True Open
then distribute in the correct quarter
That is much harder than coding “buy when A and B are true.”
So can it be coded?
Yes — parts of it can absolutely be coded.
What usually cannot be coded well is the full discretionary version of the model all at once.

That is why most traders fail here: they try to code the whole philosophy instead of one narrow executable play.

The correct way to code it
Start with one tiny model, not the whole QT universe.
Example:

Asset set: CL / RB / HO
Day: Wednesday only
Cycle: Daily cycle only
Setup: Q4 reversal only
PDArray: BPR only
Confirmation: close-based SSMT only
Entry: first reclaim/reject after SSMT
Exit: fixed target or opposite quarter objective
Now you are coding a strategy.
Before that, you are coding a religion.

What is probably stopping you right now
Not “market conditions.”
It is these 3 things:

Your rules are not fully atomic yet
Your model is too broad
Too much of the edge lives in contextual filtering
The honest answer
Your model is probably semi-systematic, not fully systematic right now.
That is normal.

A discretionary model can still be real, but a strategy needs every important judgment converted into:

exact inputs
exact conditions
exact sequence
exact invalidation
No vibes allowed.
